## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design a compact computer vision pipeline with practical on-device adaptation under tight latency constraints. It also probes understanding of data strategy and evaluation beyond single-object pose tasks.

## Key Concepts
- Lightweight detection with 2D bounding boxes
- Depth proxy estimation from object size
- Simple tracking with temporal consistency
- On-device few-shot adaptation (3-shot)
- Counting metrics and evaluation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: detection + tracking update loop
for (const frame of video) {
  const detections = detector.predict(frame);
  const depthProxy = estimateDepth(detections, calibration);
  const tracked = tracker.update(detections, depthProxy);
  yield { boxes: tracked, count: countPackages(tracked) };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a constrained, real-time CV system that reasons about 3D pose, occlusion, and actionable outcomes (feasibility of mating for tool swaps) in an edge context with new hardware revisions.

## Key Concepts
- Monocular 6-DoF pose for multiple components
- On-device adaptation (3-shot) to unseen revisions
- Occlusion-robust representation (2.5D priors)
- Tool-mating feasibility as a discriminative objective
- Synthetic+real data strategy and edge latency trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: pose and feasibility total loss
function totalLoss(posePred, poseGT, feasPred, feasGT, mask) {
  const poseDiff = posePred.sub(poseGT).mul(mask);
  const poseLoss = poseDiff.pow(2).sum() / mask.sum();
  const feasLoss = binaryCrossEntropy(feasPred, feasGT, mask);
  return poseLoss * 0.7 + feasLoss * 0.3;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
